course: everyday
lesson: 128
title: A guest at the table, and manners on the phone
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: At someone else's table you wait until food is offered, follow the family's customs, and say "No, thank you" instead of "I hate that", because a gentle refusal does not hurt the cook.
explain: Families and faiths differ: some say grace before eating, so you wait quietly; a friend fasting in Ramadan deserves kindness, never teasing; with chopsticks, you never stand them upright in rice, which looks like a funeral custom in some Asian cultures.
explain: Some foods are finger foods, like pizza, sandwiches, chips and chicken drumsticks; most others are eaten with a knife and fork.
explain: On the phone, safety comes first: you never tell a stranger you are home alone or give your address; you say your parent is busy and cannot come to the phone, and you tell your parent.
explain: A boy in Phoenix gets a call from an unknown number asking "Is your mum home?"; he lets his dad answer, because unknown callers can be scammers.
fact: Pizza, sandwiches, chips and chicken drumsticks are finger foods, and most other foods are eaten with cutlery.
fact: If a family says grace (a short prayer of thanks) before eating, you wait quietly until they finish.
fact: After a meal at a friend's house you thank the cook and offer to carry your plate.
fact: At someone else's table you wait until food is offered, or ask politely.
fact: In a restaurant you speak quietly, stay in your seat and are polite to the waiter.
fact: "No, thank you" is a kind way to refuse food you do not like.
fact: During Ramadan many Muslims do not eat or drink from dawn to sunset, and friends are kind about it.
fact: Chopsticks are never stuck upright in rice, and it is fine to ask for a fork.
fact: You never tell a caller you do not know that you are home alone, and you never give your address.
fact: Phone calls are best made between about 9 in the morning and 8 in the evening, unless it is urgent.
fact: Unknown callers could be scammers, so a parent answers or the call is ignored.
fact: Never give a caller a bank card number; a call saying you have won a prize and asking for one is a scam.
fact: On a crowded bus you speak quietly on the phone and keep it short.
fact: If a friend phones during a family meal, you ask if you may call back later.
fact: Messages can be saved, shared and misunderstood, so you never send what you would not say face to face.
exercise: Which of these is fine to eat with your hands? | A slice of pizza | A bowl of spaghetti | A plate of peas | A bowl of soup
exercise: Your friend's family says grace before dinner. What do you do? | Wait quietly until they finish | Start eating since you do not pray | Talk to your friend during it | Ask them to hurry up
exercise: You finish a meal at your friend's house. What is polite? | Say thank you to the cook and offer to carry your plate | Leave the table and go straight to play | Say thank you only if you liked the food, otherwise say nothing | Ask what is for pudding
exercise: You are hungry at a friend's house and see a plate of biscuits. What do you do? | Wait until they are offered, or ask politely | Grab a few when nobody is looking | Take the whole plate to the bedroom so you can share it later | Tell your friend to bring you some
exercise: Your family eats at a restaurant. How do you behave? | Speak quietly, stay in your seat and be polite to the waiter | Run between the tables to visit other diners and see what they ordered | Call the waiter by snapping your fingers | Shout so your family can hear you
exercise: You are offered a vegetable you dislike. What do you say? | "No, thank you" | "I hate that" | "That looks disgusting" | "Who cooked this?"
exercise: Your friend is fasting during Ramadan, and you are eating lunch. What is kind? | Ask if they mind, or eat a little apart, and never tease them | Wave your sandwich in front of them as a joke so they feel included | Tell them fasting is silly | Eat as loudly as you can
exercise: Your family eats at a Chinese restaurant with chopsticks. What should you never do? | Stick the chopsticks upright in the rice | Ask for a fork if it is too hard | Try to use the chopsticks | Rest them on the side of the plate
exercise: A stranger on the phone asks if you are home alone. What do you say? | Your parent can't come to the phone just now; then tell your parent | "Yes, I'm alone until six" | Your full address so they can visit | "Who are you? I am on my own today, so please call back later when mum is in"
exercise: When is a good time to phone a friend's house? | Between about 9 in the morning and 8 in the evening | At midnight, when everyone is home | At six in the morning, before school | Any time of day or night, since phones are always switched on
exercise: You are on the phone on a crowded bus. What is polite? | Speak quietly and keep it short | Use the speaker so you can hear better | Talk loudly so the caller hears you | Sing along to music while you talk
exercise: A friend phones during family dinner. What do you do? | Ask if you may call back later | Leave the table and talk for an hour | Talk at the table with your mouth full | Hang up without a word
exercise: Your tablet rings with a number you do not know. What is safe? | Let a parent answer, or ignore it | Answer and give your name and address | Call back many times to find out who it was | Answer and say you are home alone
exercise: You are cross with a friend and want to send a rude emoji. What is the rule? | Never send what you would not say face to face | It is fine, because emoji are only pictures and not real words | Send it and delete it quickly | Send it to a different friend instead
test: A girl in London is offered fish she does not like at her friend's house. What should she say? | "No, thank you" | "I hate fish" | "This smells bad" | Nothing, and hide it under the napkin
test: Why should children never tell an unknown caller that they are home alone? | Keeping that private keeps them safe | Because callers cannot hear children well | Because it costs more to call children | Because it is boring for the caller
test: Your Muslim friend Amir is fasting and you have crisps at break. What is a kind choice? | Ask if he minds, or eat a little apart | Offer him crisps again and again | Tease him that he looks hungry | Tell him fasting is not allowed at school
test: Why should you not stand chopsticks upright in a bowl of rice? | It looks like a funeral custom in some Asian cultures | Because the rice will spill out at once | Because chopsticks must always stay in your hand until the meal ends | Because it makes the rice go cold faster
test: A boy wants to text his friend "You're so dumb lol". What should he think about first? | Would he say it face to face? Messages can be saved and shared | Whether "lol" makes it funny enough | Whether he has enough battery | Whether the friend will read it quickly before the teacher sees it
test: A family in Texas says grace and you do not pray at home. What is respectful? | Wait quietly until they finish | Start eating to show you are different | Leave the table until they are done | Ask them to explain why they pray
test: Which of these is usually eaten with a knife and fork? | Roast chicken with gravy and vegetables | A chicken drumstick at a picnic in the park | A ham sandwich | A slice of pizza
test: A woman calls asking for your address to send you a "prize". What do you do? | Do not give it, and tell your parent | Give it, because prizes are nice | Give only your street and town | Ask her to send the prize to school instead
test: Your friend calls at 10 p.m. to ask about homework. What would be better next time? | Call earlier, between about 9 a.m. and 8 p.m. | Call even later at night, when the parents are fast asleep | Call at 6 a.m. instead | Call five times so someone answers
test: at my mate's house, they're saying grace, do i just start | Wait quietly until they finish | Start eating, you don't have to join | Talk to your friend meanwhile | Eat the bread only
test: phone rings, some guy asks are ur parents home, theyre not | Don't say you're alone and never give your address | Tell him you're home alone | Give him the address so he can come round and visit later | Tell him when they'll be back
test: my friend reckons stickin chopsticks up in your rice is fine, right? | No, it's seen as rude; lay them down, and you can ask for a fork | Yes, it shows you liked the food | Yes, that's how you keep them clean | Yes, it means you want more
test: aunt made fish, i hate fish, what do i say | No, thank you | Yuck, I hate this | Say nothing and push it off the plate | Pretend to be ill
explain: A boy in Leeds answered his mum's phone and told the caller his parents were away for the week; the caller was a stranger, and the family had to change their plans to keep the house safe.
explain: A girl in Ohio grabbed food first from every dish at a friend's house; the friend's parents did not invite her again.
fact: On the phone you never tell a stranger that you are home alone or that the family is away.
fact: Good table manners as a guest make people happy to have you back.
fact: «Loved up» means very much in love, and «wedding bells soon» means people think a couple may get married.
fact: «Lady friend» is an old-fashioned way to say girlfriend, and grown-ups' love lives are their private business.
test: phone rings, someone asks if my parents are home, i'm alone | Say they can't come to the phone and take a message | Say yes you're alone | Tell them exactly what time your parents will be back home | Give them your address
test: at my mate's for tea, gonna grab the biggest bits first | Wait to be served or take a fair share | Grab first before they're all gone, the host won't mind | Take from everyone's plate | Eat straight from the pot
test: caller says you won a prize, just tell us your mum's card number | Hang up and tell an adult, it's a scam | Read it out quickly | Give only half the number | Ask them to call back for it later when your mum is home
test: omg, Sam picked up the phone n told some random guy his parents were away all week, they say the house got robbed that night!! | Never tell callers the family is away; the robbery part may be rumour | Telling callers is fine if they sound friendly and already know your name | Every house gets robbed after a phone call | Only adults ever get scam calls
test: apparently Aisha's not eating at lunch cos of Ramadan, everyone's saying she's starving herself lol | Don't spread it; fasting in Ramadan is her family's faith, so be kind | Spread it, it's funny | Tell a teacher she's starving herself | Offer her food every single day until she gives up and eats with everyone
test: my mate said this kid stuck chopsticks straight up in his rice at a Chinese mate's house n got kicked out, true? | Probably blown up, but upright chopsticks can look like a funeral custom, so avoid it | It's certainly true and happens often | Chopsticks must always stand up in rice | Using chopsticks at all is rude for guests
test: at dinner auntie asks "so, any girlfriend yet?" and your big cousin goes red and mumbles. what does she mean and what's polite for you? | She is asking if he's going out with someone; let him answer and don't tease | She wants to know his best friend, so shout a name | She's asking about his job, so say he's lazy | It's a joke question, so keep repeating it all dinner
test: on the phone mum laughs "oh they're properly loved up, those two, wedding bells soon" — you ask what that means | The couple are very much in love and may get married | Two people got locked up and need help | Somebody bought new bells for a church | Two people are ill and staying in bed
test: a guest at the table says "and how's your dad's lady friend?" and everyone goes quiet. what's the best thing for a kid to do? | Leave it to the grown-ups and not make it a joke; ask mum later if you want | Answer with everything you know about her | Laugh and say «she's not a lady» | Ask the guest why it's so quiet
exam: Identify the appropriate conduct where a host family says grace before a meal. | Wait quietly until they have finished | Begin eating so as not to waste time | Leave the table until grace is over | Ask them to say grace more quickly
exam: It is given that a caller unknown to you asks whether your parents are at home. Select the response consistent with safety. | Say your parent is busy and cannot come to the phone | Say you are home alone and give your address | Say the whole family is away for the week | Ask the caller to come round in person
exam: Which of the following constitutes a courteous refusal of food one does not like? | "No, thank you." | "I hate that." | "That looks awful." | "Take it away now."
exam: Ascertain which of the following foods are customarily eaten with the fingers. | Pizza, sandwiches, chips and chicken drumsticks | Soup, spaghetti, salad and mashed potato | Roast beef, rice, peas and gravy | Porridge, custard, stew and jelly
